{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-44097",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2}=(a-b)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n25{x}^{2}-20x+4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x)^{2}-20x+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x)^{2}-2(10x)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(10x)$ as a product of $5x$ and $2:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x)^{2}-2(5x\\cdot 2)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=5x$ and $b=2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
